{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_05c1f8ec2cd440cc0169bb4532d534819186ce6756f300c345", "created": 1773880626, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Colorectal Cancer Care Pathway: Colectomy, Colonoscopy, Surveillance, and APR\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community is a clinical knowledge network centered on colorectal cancer evaluation and management, connecting diagnostic and biomarker testing, surgical and endoscopic interventions, and structured post-treatment surveillance. Key treatment nodes include colectomy and tumor resection (often followed by stage-dependent adjuvant chemotherapy) and, for locally progressive or recurrent anal/low rectal disease, radical surgery such as abdominoperineal resection (APR) with permanent colostomy creation. Diagnostic and monitoring components include colonoscopy with polypectomy, mismatch repair (MMR)/microsatellite instability testing of tumor tissue, and multi-modality surveillance (follow-up schedules, digital rectal exams, imaging when indicated, and endoscopic/ultrasound approaches). The network is framed as patient-guideline-oriented (NCCN Guidelines for Patients) and includes discussion of procedural complications such as bleeding and infection, emphasizing follow-up and recurrence detection as organizing principles. [Data: Entities (2659, 2649, 4398, 2119, 2167); Relationships (9289, 9239, 9521, 3726, 14247, +more)].\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The community is organized as an end-to-end colorectal cancer workflow from testing to treatment planning\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A central organizing concept is “Testing and Treatment Planning,” described as a process that compiles diagnostic, risk, and biomarker information to create an individualized plan. This explicitly includes tumor biomarker testing and other pre-treatment evaluations (e.g., blood tests